.TH INIT 8
.SH NAME
init \- the supervisor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B init
(started by the kernel)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B init
is the only program the kernel starts: the first boot module, the
"supervisor". It starts the servers of its table, starts each again when
it fails, and starts some only when they are needed. It alone may give
device rights
.RB ( SPAWN_DRIVER )
and start the other boot modules, and it adopts every process whose
parent ended.
.PP
At boot:
.B con
(the console),
.B usb
(USB keys, keyboards, mice),
.B vblk
(the disk),
.B fs
(the files),
.B login
(the console's prompt),
.B atlas
(the desktop),
.B bootlog
.RB ( "dmesg \-w" ),
.BR wifi ,
.BR bt ,
.BR sshd ,
.B gfxhat
and on x86-64
.BR acpi ,
.BR i2chid ,
.BR ipts .
A service with nothing to drive ends with 0 and is not started again.
.PP
On demand: a service with a port is not started at boot. init declares
the port's name to the kernel
.RB ( SYS_WANT );
the first program that looks it up waits, and init starts the server:
.B auth
(port
.BR auth ),
.B vnet
.RB ( nic0 ),
.B netd
.RB ( net ),
.B unixd
.RB ( unix ),
.B gpio
.RB ( gpio ),
.B audio
.RB ( audio ),
.B sia
.RB ( sia ).
After its idle time unused, init asks the server "may you stop?"
.RB ( SVC_MAYSTOP );
one that holds no client state says yes, ends, and is marked
.BR idle .
.PP
Failures: the kernel tells init when a child ends
.RB ( NOTE_CHILD ).
It starts it again at once, unless it failed within a second of
starting: then it waits 10 ms, 20 ms, 40 ms ... up to 1 s, and gives up
after 5 such failures in a row. A watcher pings the core servers every
10 s
.RB ( SVC_PING );
one that has not answered after 30 s is ended, and so started again.
.PP
The boot modules' images stay in the kernel
.RB ( SPAWN_MODULE ):
a crashed disk driver comes back even though the disk is unreachable
without it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svc (8),
.BR boot (7),
.BR login (8),
.BR auth (8)
